  "content": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Hello there! In our last lessons, we explored hashing and how to quickly look up items. Today, we are going to learn a simple but incredibly powerful trick called **Prefix Sum**. We already know how arrays store elements side-by-side in memory and how we can access any element instantly if we know its index. Today, we will use this knowledge to learn how to prepare arrays for fast computations."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "what-is-prefix-sum",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1. What is Prefix Sum?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Let's start by breaking down the name. **Prefix** means 'at the beginning', and **Sum** means 'adding things together'. Put them together, and a **Prefix Sum** is a new array where each slot stores the sum of all elements from the very beginning of the original array up to that current slot."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Let's define it using a concrete example of an input array A and its corresponding output prefix sum array P:"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "id": "prefix-sum-intro-drawing"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "how-to-build-it",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "2. How to Build It"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "How do we construct the prefix sum array? We can build it in **O(N)** time by reusing our previous calculations!"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The key idea is: **The sum up to the current index is equal to the sum up to the previous index plus the current element.**"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "style": "important",
      "text": "Formula to build prefix sum:\n`P[i] = P[i - 1] + A[i]`  (for i > 0)"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "id": "prefix-sum-building-drawing"
    },
    {
